    <label>LWC Dynamic UI</label>
    <protected>false</protected>
    <values>
        <field>Feature_Key__c</field>
        <value xsi:type="xsd:string">LWC_DYNAMIC_UI</value>
    </values>
    <values>
        <field>Prompt_Content__c</field>
        <value
      xsi:type="xsd:string"
    ><![CDATA[
== DYNAMIC UI GENERATION ==
Use when: the ticket needs conditional rendering, loops over a list, a
reusable/generic component, or a layout that adapts to screen size.

-- CONDITIONAL RENDERING: lwc:if/lwc:elseif/lwc:else (PREFERRED) --
Use the lwc:if/lwc:elseif/lwc:else directive family for new components —
this is the current recommended LWC syntax:

  <template>
      <template lwc:if={isOpen}>
          <p>Status: Open</p>
      </template>
      <template lwc:elseif={isPending}>
          <p>Status: Pending</p>
      </template>
      <template lwc:else>
          <p>Status: Closed</p>
      </template>
  </template>

RULES:
- lwc:elseif and lwc:else must directly follow the lwc:if that starts the
  chain (or another lwc:elseif continuing it) — never a standalone
  lwc:elseif/lwc:else with no preceding lwc:if.
- Each condition binding is a bare property or getter reference, per the
  standard {...} binding rule already given in the base LWC guidance — never
  an inline expression like lwc:if={status === 'Open'}. Expose a getter
  (get isOpen() { return this.status === 'Open'; }) and bind to that instead.

-- LEGACY if:true / if:false --
The older if:true={cond} / if:false={cond} directives still work and are
fine to reuse when MERGING into an existing component that already uses
them (do not churn a component's existing working style just to modernize
it) — but prefer lwc:if/lwc:elseif/lwc:else for anything newly generated.

CRITICAL TRUTHINESS GOTCHA: if:true={someList} (or lwc:if={someList}) is
TRUE even when someList is an EMPTY array — an array is a truthy value in
JavaScript regardless of its length. Never bind a conditional directly to
an array or a @wire result:
  WRONG:  <template if:true={contacts}>          <!-- true even if contacts = [] -->
  RIGHT:  get hasContacts() { return this.contacts && this.contacts.length > 0; }
          <template lwc:if={hasContacts}>
This applies to any "show this section only if there is data" requirement —
always expose a boolean getter, never bind the directive to the raw
array/collection/wire-result property itself.

-- LOOPS: for:each AND iterator:x --
for:each — the common case, one variable per item:

  <template for:each={contacts} for:item="contact">
      <div key={contact.Id}>{contact.Name}</div>
  </template>

iterator:x — use only when the ticket needs to style the first/last row
differently (e.g. a divider between rows, no divider after the last one):

  <template iterator:it={contacts}>
      <div key={it.value.Id}>
          {it.value.Name}
          <template lwc:if={it.last}>(last)</template>
      </div>
  </template>

RULES:
- ALWAYS put a key={uniqueField} on the element directly inside the loop
  (typically key={item.Id}) — LWC requires a unique key per iterated element
  for its rendering diff algorithm; omitting it is rejected at deploy.
- NEVER combine a conditional directive (lwc:if/lwc:elseif/lwc:else,
  if:true/if:false) and an iterator directive (for:each, iterator:x) on the
  SAME <template> tag — this is illegal and rejected at deploy. Nest two
  separate <template> tags instead: one for the condition, one for the loop.
- it.first / it.last (iterator:x only) are booleans, bind them directly:
  <template lwc:if={it.first}>...</template> — never it.first === true.

-- REUSABLE / GENERIC COMPONENTS --
A "reusable component" ticket wants a component with NO hardcoded business
logic — configuration comes in entirely through @api properties, and content
comes in through slots, not through object/field names baked into the JS:

  Generic JS (genericCard.js):
    import { LightningElement, api } from 'lwc';
    export default class GenericCard extends LightningElement {
        @api title;
        @api iconName;
    }

  Generic HTML (genericCard.html):
    <template>
        <lightning-card title={title} icon-name={iconName}>
            <slot></slot>
        </lightning-card>
    </template>

  Consumer HTML (uses the generic component with specific content):
    <c-generic-card title="Open Cases" icon-name="standard:case">
        <p>{caseCount} open cases</p>
    </c-generic-card>

RULES:
- A generic/reusable component never imports an Apex controller or a
  specific SObject's schema fields directly — those belong in the
  component that CONSUMES it, passed down via @api properties.
- Prefer a default (unnamed) <slot></slot> for arbitrary consumer content;
  use a named slot (<slot name="footer"></slot>) only when the ticket
  describes more than one distinct content region.
- CRITICAL — WHEN THE TICKET NAMES A SPECIFIC FIRST USE (e.g. "build a
  reusable X, first use: wrap Y on Z"), you MUST generate BOTH components
  as separate 3-file bundles in the same response: (1) the generic/
  reusable component itself (@api properties + <slot>, no business logic),
  AND (2) the consumer component that actually uses it — fetches/computes
  the real data, sets the generic component's @api attributes, and passes
  the real content into its slot. Never stop after generating only the
  generic shell — a ticket describing a specific first use is asking for
  that use to exist, not just the reusable building block.
- NEVER treat the bare generic/reusable component itself as the thing to
  place on a page. It has no default title, icon, or slot content — placed
  alone it renders an empty, useless card. If the ticket asks for page
  placement, the component intended for that placement is always the
  CONSUMER (the one with the real title/icon/content), never the generic
  shell it wraps.

-- RESPONSIVE LAYOUTS: SLDS GRID --
Use SLDS grid utility classes for any "responsive"/"adapts to screen size"
requirement — never hand-rolled CSS media queries for a plain multi-column
layout the SLDS grid system already covers:

  <div class="slds-grid slds-wrap slds-gutters">
      <div class="slds-col slds-size_1-of-1 slds-medium-size_1-of-2 slds-large-size_1-of-3">
          <!-- one card -->
      </div>
  </div>

RULES:
- slds-grid on the container, slds-wrap so columns drop to the next row
  instead of overflowing, slds-gutters for spacing between columns.
- slds-size_1-of-N is the DEFAULT (smallest/mobile) column width; add
  slds-medium-size_1-of-N and/or slds-large-size_1-of-N to widen the column
  at larger breakpoints — always specify the small/default size first.
]]></value>
